#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096302 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "660e7de2-aeea-55ad-971b-3c137ccb1521" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change Given a Related Rate and an Explicit Function"}] :question/id #uuid "1e131c11-ded0-52dc-866f-3073eac4565a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10899" :question/problem "Given that $y=x^3+e^x$ for $x=x(t)$, and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=2$ at the point where $x=1$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x^{3} + e^{x} \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(y) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(x^{3} + e^{x}) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3} + e^{x}) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot (3x^{2} + e^{x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=2\n$$\n\nand $x=1$, and solve for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot (3 \\cdot 1^{2} + e^{1}) \\\\\n2 &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot (3 + e) \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{2}{3 + e}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096303 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f2818fc-787e-5b46-8a7a-c5fbea7905a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Angular Rate Given a Related Rate"}] :question/id #uuid "fdf149d5-0139-5291-ab72-c070fb6a4a6e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10901" :question/problem "Given that $r=\\sin\\theta$ for $\\theta = \\theta(t)$, and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}=\\sqrt{2}$ at the point where $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\theta}{\\textrm d t}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\sin θ \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(r) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin θ) \\\\\n\\frac{dr}{dt} &= \\frac{dθ}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dθ}(\\sin θ) \\\\\n\\frac{dr}{dt} &= \\frac{dθ}{dt} \\cdot \\cos θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}=\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand solve for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\theta}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dr}{dt} &= \\frac{dθ}{dt} \\cdot \\cos θ \\\\\n\\sqrt{2} &= \\frac{dθ}{dt} \\cdot \\cos (\\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n\\sqrt{2} &= \\frac{dθ}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{dθ}{dt} &= \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n\\frac{dθ}{dt} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096304 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b47a8b7-722d-564e-8e7a-ad07577d6f6b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Rate of Change of an XY-Coordinate"}] :question/id #uuid "1b0131b8-bc82-5547-9b83-95f2b3e53447" :question/math-academy-id "e-10902" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is moving along the curve $y=x+e^x$ such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is changing at the rate of $2 \\,\\textrm{cm}/\\textrm{s}$. Find the rate of change of the $y$-coordinate when $x=5\\,\\textrm{cm}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x + e^{x} \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dt}(y) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(x + e^{x}) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x + e^{x}) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\cdot (1 + e^{x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can substitute the given information\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=2 \\,\\textrm{cm}/\\textrm{s}\n$$\n\nand $x=5$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= 2(1 + e^{5})cm/s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "660e7de2-aeea-55ad-971b-3c137ccb1521" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change Given a Related Rate and an Explicit Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82af1229-8171-53dc-abcf-4165fa77d4c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095390} {:answer/id #uuid "9142e4ee-002c-5474-b2ad-6a49ebe6e83f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095391} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e19d83-a825-5642-8fb3-bcf22b57de41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095392} {:answer/id #uuid "2251d5a1-9b92-54be-a4e6-0fcb0d019f33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095393} {:answer/id #uuid "27990112-1922-59d6-b566-f6926b995a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82af1229-8171-53dc-abcf-4165fa77d4c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095390} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c1a3cef-0549-5117-8a5a-ea6aa5fc7cbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095389}] :question/id #uuid "123374e6-ecc1-51ff-9e7a-07d1d48a742d" :question/math-academy-id "q-48765" :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nGiven that $y = 2x^{3} - 2x$ for $x = x(t)$, and that $\\frac{dx}{dt} = 1$ at the point where $x =-2$, find $\\frac{dy}{dt}$." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f2818fc-787e-5b46-8a7a-c5fbea7905a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Angular Rate Given a Related Rate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a75c02bc-adc8-5003-b427-7b5c1521abb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095408} {:answer/id #uuid "ae751400-4a7b-5218-93d5-fefd562175f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095409} {:answer/id #uuid "c023b6d8-a5a8-53a7-87a5-b2c274159bbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095410} {:answer/id #uuid "3fbbbe17-86a4-5c10-8f88-cd816fe1ab5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095411} {:answer/id #uuid "7dcb5ab3-421c-5703-88a2-4df7c7550815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a75c02bc-adc8-5003-b427-7b5c1521abb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095408} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5753917-2a28-59b2-b50e-0f0413227d87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095407}] :question/id #uuid "e52c46bb-53c6-584c-86da-75775eac460d" :question/math-academy-id "q-48806" :question/problem "Given that $r = \\cos θ$ for $θ = θ(t)$, and that $\\frac{dr}{dt} = \\sqrt{2}$ at the point where $θ = \\frac{π}{4}$, find $\\frac{dθ}{dt}$."}]}
